---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="CritPages | Chris Dabel"
  description="A critique app for unpublished writing, built solo on Expo and Supabase. What it is, what I decided, and why the access model lives in the database."
>
  <h1>CritPages</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The critique app for unpublished writing. Founder and sole engineer, in
    beta since mid 2026.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
    <p>
      Writers share pages with a critique group, a beta-reader round, or a
      single partner. Readers mark them up on their phones: a tag, a strike, a
      heart, or a comment attached to a word, a sentence, or a paragraph, each
      in about two taps. Each reader sees only their own marks. The writer sees
      all of them together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Groups can read live, everyone following along and marking on their own
      device while one person reads aloud. Pages are drawn rather than
      rendered as selectable text, so a manuscript cannot be copied out. Access
      is by invitation, and a writer can remove a reader or end a round.
    </p>
    <p>
      Native apps for iPhone and Android, with the web handling invitations,
      accounts, finding beta readers, and scheduling sessions. One-on-one use
      is free. Groups and beta rounds are paid, and free during the beta.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="decisions">
    <h2 id="decisions">Decisions</h2>
    <dl class="decisions">
      <div>
        <dt>Access control lives in the database</dt>
        <dd>
          Who can see a page is decided by Postgres row-level security
          policies in Supabase, not by application code. An author's
          unpublished work is visible only to their critique group, and that
          holds for every client and every query path. It is the same
          discipline as the row access policies I built for a fintech's
          multi-tenant warehouse, applied to a product.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>One codebase, both phones</dt>
        <dd>
          React Native with Expo, shipping to iOS and Android from a single
          codebase. For one engineer, that is the difference between a product
          and a prototype.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The web does what phones are bad at</dt>
        <dd>
          Invitations, accounts, finding beta readers, and scheduling live
          sessions run on a Next.js site on Vercel. The reading and marking
          stay native, where two taps on a sentence feel right.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Feedback is independent</dt>
        <dd>
          Readers never see each other's marks while they read, so nobody
          anchors on the first opinion. The writer gets the full set, and the
          disagreements are the useful part.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The page looks like a page</dt>
        <dd>
          No spellcheck underlines, no track-changes clutter. The reading
          surface is a clean copy of the manuscript, and the marks sit on top
          of it.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="status">
    <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
    <p>
      Pre-launch beta. Early access is open by email to a limited number of
      groups, beta rounds, and pairs at
      <a href="https://critpages.com">critpages.com</a>. Product notes go out
      on <a href="https://critpages.substack.com">the CritPages Substack</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .decisions { margin: 0; }
  .decisions div { padding: 1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .decisions dt { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .decisions dd { margin: 0; }
</style>
